WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.155 --> 00:00:15.405
MODERATOR: More or less
annual event in the Department

00:00:15.405 --> 00:00:16.970
of Aeronautics and
Astronomics, which

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.950
was made possible
by the [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.680
Major Lester Gardner.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.270
It's an annual lecture
on history of aerospace.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:31.380
Gardner was a graduate of the
class of '98, which we now

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.610
have to distinguish
by saying 1898,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.470
as the Institute has now cycled
through more than a century.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.680
And as best I can figure
out, he was basically

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.530
a buddy of [? Hunziker's. ?]
Is that right?

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.020
Does anyone remember
this far back?

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.350
[? Hunziker ?] seemed to
have this network of buddies

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.100
who were, A, influential,
and B, had money.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.400
That's the image
that comes across.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.580
And Gardner was one of them.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.620
He published magazine
for a number of years

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:05.489
called Aviation and
Aeronautical Engineer.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.840
And he was a decorated aviation
officer in World War I,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.970
and gave us the fund that
allows the annual sponsorship

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.870
of this lecture, which is a
prestigious list of lectures.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.260
I'll just read a few off
of the previous lectures.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.260
Sikorsky, Doolittle, Sperry,
Otto Koppen, the two tailors,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.490
[? Abe Silverstein, ?]
[? Doc Draper, ?] Hans

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:37.050
[INAUDIBLE],, Bill Sears,
Gerhard Neumann, T. Wilson,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:41.250
Neil Armstrong, and Bob Seamans,
and most recently, René Miller.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.070
And we're indeed
honored today to have

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.620
one of our alumnus, Joe Gavin.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.030
Not only alumnus, a
very active supporter

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.230
in [INAUDIBLE] department.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.920
Joe received his SB in 1841.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.320
A member of the
famous class of '41

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.690
that included Jimmy [? Marr ?]
and a few other people who were

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.130
quite active in the department.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.256
Who else was in
the class of '41?

00:02:06.256 --> 00:02:07.417
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLES]

00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:08.750
MODERATOR: It's a long time ago.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.042
AUDIENCE: Long time ago.

00:02:11.042 --> 00:02:13.040
MODERATOR: About 55 years ago.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.750
He was in the Naval
Reserve and served

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.720
in the Bureau of Aeronautics
during World War II.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.410
He was a project officer for
the Navy's first jet fighter.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.910
He joined Grumman after the
war as a design engineer,

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.280
and he served in many
capacities there,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.350
including the one in which he
will talk about [? us ?] today.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.570
That is, he was a vice
president and director

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:39.480
of the Lunar Module Program
during the Apollo era.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.500
Served there until his
retirement in 1985.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.110
Outside of Grumman, he's
had a lot of influence

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.710
on the nation and the world.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.210
He's a member of the
AIAA since 1946, which

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.240
was before it was the AIAA.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.840
In 1982, he was the president
of our professional society,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.300
and became the honorary
fellow [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.170
[? honorary ?] fellow in 1984.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.630
He's received a NASA
Distinguished Public Service

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:09.200
Medal, and was a member
of the National Academy.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.960
He's a member of the
International Academy

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:17.870
of Astronautics, and he
was a director of the AAAS,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.230
and became a fellow
of that in 1991.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.430
And in his spare time, he's been
active here at the Institute

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.740
as a member of the
Educational Council.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.410
Was the president of
the Alumni Association.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.340
Has served on a number
of visiting committees,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.560
including ours.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.720
Is a life member
of the Corporation,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.300
and has served on its
executive committee, which

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.950
is the seat of real power
in MIT, from 1984 to 1991.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.830
So I would like to--

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.930
I would have the honor,
in fact, to introduce

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.045
Joe to give his address
on the technology

00:03:50.045 --> 00:03:53.302
and programmatic development
of the lunar module.

00:03:53.302 --> 00:03:54.010
GAVIN: Thank you.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.500
Well, it's a very
considerable honor

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.680
to join the group who
have given this lecture.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.019
It also brings back a rather
strong pang of nostalgia.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.880
Now, I have to explain that it's
a little hard to feel nostalgic

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.060
about 10 years of
sheer desperation,

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.490
because that's
about what it was.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:30.510
And I'm just going to hit
some of the high points here,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.710
and I'll skip some of
the real difficult times.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:44.040
But this program, the Apollo
program, started as a result,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.170
I think, of the competition
in the Cold War.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.430
There were many things
that led up to it,

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:57.050
and I would suggest that
the beginnings of NASA

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:00.840
and the beginnings of Apollo
are worth reading up on,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.730
because they're
quite complicated.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:06.660
In my view, one or
two major things.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.460
Mr. Eisenhower
decided that NASA was

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.920
going to be an open,
unclassified agency.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.750
He managed to fend off the
Air Force, who wanted the job.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:26.655
And then, of course, Mr. Kennedy
made the commitment to do it.

00:05:26.655 --> 00:05:29.820
And at the time that he
made that commitment,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.630
one of his advisors
was saying that,

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.970
with the reliability
available [? and ?]

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:41.730
components at that time, that
it would take probably 40

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:42.390
landings--

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:47.610
40 attempts to make
a successful landing.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:51.750
Well, it turned out
otherwise, of course.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:56.550
And the credit really has to
go to an awful lot of people.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:02.250
I'm here today to tell you, as I
remember it, about the program.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.900
But there are an
awful lot of people

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.310
who were involved at Grumman
and at subcontractors

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.860
at the various NASA centers.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:16.800
And it's pretty hard to
find any group, I think,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.840
that worked as diligently
and in as focused

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.210
a fashion for those 10 years.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:28.975
Well, now let's get
into the slides.

00:06:38.061 --> 00:06:43.000
This particular slide is
from Apollo 15, as I recall.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.090
And the reason for showing
it is, first of all,

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.980
it is on the lunar surface.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:52.300
And secondly, the
rover is in sight.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:57.160
And the last three lunar
modules had the ability

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.870
to carry the rover.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.230
They had more oxygen,
more battery power,

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.212
and could stay longer.

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:05.920
But let's go back and
see how it started.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.130
The command and service
module got started,

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:17.080
but it was many months later
that the decision was finally

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.880
made to embrace lunar
orbit rendezvous.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.880
This was something that
John Houbolt promoted.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.150
And looking back at
it, I don't think

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.835
we would have gotten there
without that concept.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:40.590
So that was finally embraced,
I think, in July of '62.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:44.097
And then there
was a competition.

00:07:44.097 --> 00:07:45.680
And it was an
interesting competition.

00:07:48.302 --> 00:07:53.510
The center in Houston
asked 20 questions.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.670
And you had to answer
those 20 questions

00:07:55.670 --> 00:08:01.700
in not over 100 pages, and with
print not below a certain size.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.880
And the idea was that if you
knew what you were doing,

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.130
it would show in the
answers to those questions.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.870
Well, we did submit a proposal.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.500
I'm not sure that our
then-senior management

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.030
expected that we would
ever win anything.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:24.860
But we had to have a
strong design in order

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.560
to answer the questions.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.420
And this is what
we had at the time.

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.260
Now, this was a
vehicle that was going

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:38.510
to be considerably lighter than
what eventually came out of it.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.360
And it had,
interestingly enough,

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.170
five landing legs on the
premise that if you broke one,

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.690
it wouldn't tip over.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.940
The other thing that's
interesting about this

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.900
is that there were
two docking hatches.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.590
And we had reason to believe
that that was an advantage.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:02.990
We had a helicopter-like cabin.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:06.500
That didn't last long, but
the idea of good visibility

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.750
was there.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.430
And I'll show you.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.660
And this shows that there's
an aft equipment bay.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.510
Well, we did have an
aft equipment bay.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.290
It didn't quite look like that.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:26.410
And then we'll jump right
away to what finally came out.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:30.650
And this was taken
from Apollo 9,

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.770
the flight test in Earth orbit.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.680
And it's really upside
down, but it's easier

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.750
to understand this way.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.080
And here, you can see the
probes on the landing gear.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:47.150
There's great concern that the
dust on the surface of the moon

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.070
would obscure
things to the point

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:54.740
where you needed some mechanical
indication of landing.

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:58.760
This also shows the exhaust
[? valve ?] of the descent

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:05.870
engine, and the rather
massive insulation around it.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:09.280
Up at the top is the
rendezvous radar.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.870
There was a flasher
here, which was

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.630
a backup means of rendezvous.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:18.800
And in this picture, you really
can't see the two windows,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.540
because what we have done
in developing the cockpit

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:28.850
arrangement was to turn the
front of the vehicle inside out

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:33.540
and pull the windows right
back to the astronaut's eye.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:39.140
Now, we hadn't been into this
contract for more than three

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:41.870
months when we began
to understand what

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:43.950
some of the priorities were.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:48.560
Now, this was a "cost plus
incentive fee" contract.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.210
And the incentives
depended on performance--

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.750
which turned out to
be largely weight--

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.810
schedule, and cost.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.970
Well, I think in
about three months,

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:07.010
it came to us that those
things were not equal.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.980
Performance was
clearly number one.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:12.980
We do our best to
meet the schedule.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.370
And I hate to say this,
Joe, but we really

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:20.675
left the cost to come
third, as he well knows.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.410
But that was an important idea.

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:34.070
And it took us about two years
to firm up the final design,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:37.490
because at the time we
were in the competition,

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.160
we did not know, for
example, the details

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:44.690
of the MIT-designed
guidance system.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:47.390
And then, of course,
there were requirements

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:51.290
like a backup guidance
system and so on.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:52.160
Let's go on.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.370
I'm not sure which
mission this came from,

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.400
but this was the ascent stage.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.180
And I picked this
slide particularly

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:07.430
to show the rendezvous
radar, the front door--

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:11.570
the front docking
hatch that we proposed

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.570
became a door, not
a docking hatch.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:19.070
And then this was the
ascent engine exhaust here.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.780
And this gives you
a pretty good idea

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:26.450
of where the propellant tanks
for the ascent engine were.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:34.100
It also shows clearly the quad
reaction control thrusters.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:39.080
There were four on each of the
four corners of the vehicle.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:42.610
It's also pretty
clear that this is not

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.030
a very aerodynamic vehicle.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:49.210
To a group of
airplane designers,

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:53.320
this was a real
awakening, that we did not

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:58.600
have to worry about
conventional aerodynamics.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:03.550
And it became very
utilitarian in appearance,

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:07.615
and only its designers
could love it.

00:13:07.615 --> 00:13:09.940
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLING]

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:16.780
GAVIN: Now, at the
time that we started,

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.520
not much was known
about the lunar surface.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:23.920
In fact, there was a well-known
astrophysicist from Cornell

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.170
who said, look,
there's going to be

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:31.570
10 meters of impalpable
dust, and not only will you

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:34.360
sink into it, but
electrostatically, it's

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.490
going to swarm right
up over the vehicle.

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:42.400
Well, I think he told me
that in 1965, by which time

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:45.760
we were reasonably
committed to the design,

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:47.680
and we didn't buy it.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.510
And fortunately, he was wrong.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:55.120
But we did have a pretty
conservative approach

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:00.160
to landing, as you can see by
reading some of these things.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:07.990
And vertical 10 feet per
second is a pretty good jolt.

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:10.150
Now, if you land on
an aircraft carrier,

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.730
you worry about maybe
20 feet per second.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:17.135
But for a land-based airplane,
that's not too unreasonable.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:23.920
But since we didn't
know about the surface,

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.550
we made some very
broad assumptions.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.920
We assumed everything
from landing

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:33.370
on the equivalent
of ice to landing

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:37.900
in four small craters
ideally located

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:41.890
to trap all four landing gears.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:47.800
And we landed with
various pitching motions,

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:54.070
rolling motions, yawing
motions, uphill, downhill.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:58.390
And 400 runs later, we
thought we had conservatively

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:00.590
surrounded this problem.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:05.560
And as it turned out much later,
we probably had over-designed.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.270
Now, one of the things
that occurred to us--

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:16.440
not at the very beginning,
but into it a few months--

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:21.360
was that this vehicle only
has to make one landing.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.670
Now, that's a revelation
too to somebody

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:26.400
who's been designing airplanes.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.790
And so we decided that--

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:33.000
oh, well, this is the
tip-over condition

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.050
that we worried about.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:44.230
So the shock-absorbing
material is aluminum honeycomb

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.520
in a billet.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:47.650
And it's good for one landing.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:50.600
It will take the shock
and eat up the energy.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:55.390
It does not rebound, and you
can't use it a second time,

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:59.300
but of course, we're not
using it a second time.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:03.230
Now, we-- in conventional
aircraft fashion--

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:09.260
ran some drop tests as soon
as we had a vehicle to drop.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:13.210
And we hoisted up in the hangar
and got all the conditions

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.590
just right, and let it go.

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:17.560
And it came down
with a crash that

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:20.980
was absolutely
frightening, because all

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:27.220
of the gauges in this machine
were much lighter than anything

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:30.130
we had experienced in aircraft.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.750
And I'm going to say a
little bit about that later.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.350
But it made a terrible noise.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:39.670
And it made us so
worried at a later date

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:43.540
about the flexibility
that, much later, we

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:47.230
insisted on a drop
test where we had

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:51.040
all of the wiring and
electronics in the vehicle

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.530
to see whether the
flexibility of the vehicle

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:58.900
would interfere with
the electrical systems.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:03.490
Actually, we didn't find any
electrical system problems,

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.880
but we did find one
small mechanical problem

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:07.900
that we had to fix.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:21.930
Now, I'll add a few more
points about the landing gear.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:27.780
We provided the ability
to absorb something

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:31.030
like 30 inches of [? travel ?]
in the landing gear.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:34.290
I don't think any of
the actual landings used

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.390
more than 3 inches.

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:39.480
And by the time we
got to this point

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:43.110
[? that ?] we knew a little bit
more about the lunar surface,

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:45.120
the astronauts had
spent a lot of time

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.920
in the simulator figuring
out how to fly it.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.800
And what they really
wound up doing

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:59.040
was to set a known rate of
descent on the autopilot,

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:04.230
and then fly the machine
manually in the other planes.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.160
And that worked very well.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:09.360
And I think, as a conclusion,
we can say the landing gear was

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.400
somewhat over-designed.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:13.290
But this also shows
one other thing.

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:18.990
It shows that there's ample
clearance between the descent

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:23.650
rocket engine exhaust
bell and the surface.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:25.830
And this became
critical later when

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:31.800
we added the lunar rover and the
extended stay time provisions,

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.860
because we then had
a heavier vehicle.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:41.580
And in order to get there with
the same amount of propellant,

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:45.420
we had to extend this
bell a few inches.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:52.080
And it turned out that that
would work, and was acceptable,

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.471
and that's what we did.

00:18:54.471 --> 00:18:57.450
Now, the other thing that
came out of these landings

00:18:57.450 --> 00:19:01.590
is that, unlike what
happens on the ground,

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:05.430
on Earth here, when a vertical
landing machine comes down,

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:07.740
you have dust that
starts out sideways,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:10.170
and then pretty soon, you
have billows coming up

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:12.735
because of the interaction
with the atmosphere.

00:19:12.735 --> 00:19:15.540
Well, on the moon,
there is no atmosphere.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:18.810
And what seems to happen
is that the dust takes off

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:23.790
horizontally, and just
goes out of the picture,

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:28.050
so that the dust that
we worried about earlier

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:29.910
wasn't that much of a problem.

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:37.795
Now, one of our worst problems--

00:19:37.795 --> 00:19:42.490
it was with us every
day for 10 years--

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:46.850
was the tendency of
the vehicle to grow.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:52.720
And I think the
significant point

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:56.680
is from about here, when
the design was finally

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.000
pretty much finalized.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.893
And then we had
the weight growth.

00:20:01.893 --> 00:20:04.840
And then we had a huge effort--

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:07.720
huge in numbers of man hours--

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.520
to squeeze the weight out of it.

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:15.760
And then over here in early
'67 was the fire at the Cape,

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:22.240
and that led to some tests
with an iron monster cabin

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:28.840
and a variety of other things
to ensure that fire would not

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:34.980
occur inside the reduced
pressure of a pure oxygen

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:36.710
atmosphere in the cabin.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:39.980
And so the weight
went back up again.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:43.600
And then, of course, this
represents the addition

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.770
of the rover and the longer
stay time provisions.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:57.060
To give you some idea of what
the fireproofing amounted to,

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:01.660
this is the back of a
circuit breaker panel.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:06.370
And we have little baggies--

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:09.060
well, we found, of course,
that the circuit breaker

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:12.060
housings themselves would burn.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.850
So then, what to do?

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:16.410
So we created these
little baggies

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:22.310
that went around that
circuit breaker body.

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:26.030
And then all of the other
electrical connections

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:30.140
were covered with this
mud-like material.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:34.310
I thought I'd never forget
its name, but I have.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:40.030
And it was a very
extensive redesign,

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:46.300
and I would say that we lost
about a year at that point.

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:57.220
To continue with
the weight story,

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:04.590
this is a picture of the ascent
stage tanks that are spherical.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:06.700
They're very thin.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:10.500
Proportionally, they're
thinner than egg shells.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:18.290
And they were designed on the
basis of fracture mechanics

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:23.060
to be good for no more
than 100 pressurizations.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:28.760
The propellants were fed
to the engines by pressure.

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:31.590
The pressure was helium.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:36.230
And so every tank
had a pedigree.

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:39.310
Every test that was
made was recorded.

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:43.910
And we made sure that before
it went off on a mission,

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:47.000
that there were an ample
number of cycles left.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:55.030
And this is a view of
the descent stage tank.

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:59.470
And this is
similarly thin stuff.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:07.120
In fact, we did a lot of our
testing with referee fluids--

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:10.120
in other words, fluids that
had the characteristics

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:17.370
of the nitrogen
tetraoxide and the UDNH,

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:20.920
but which were not
nasty to handle.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:24.990
And we did a lot of the
testing with heavyweight tanks.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:30.040
When we received our first
lightweight descent stage

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:33.900
tank, of course, we filled it.

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:35.800
Checked the volume.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:42.370
And consternation set in
because it held 20 seconds more

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:46.250
propellant than
we had counted on.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:49.510
And so then the question was,
did somebody make a mistake?

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.040
Well, no, they hadn't.

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:53.650
What happened was that under
the weight of the propellant,

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.570
the tank stretched.

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:59.590
And we were very happy to have
that extra 20 seconds' worth,

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:06.100
because if you remember
Armstrong's first landing,

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:10.570
he hesitated a bit to
clear some boulders that he

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:12.130
was coming in on.

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:14.500
And I think he was down
to about 20 seconds

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:17.320
at the time he touched down.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:18.940
The rest [? of ?]
mission control

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:20.200
were blue at that point.

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:30.660
And while we're
talking about tanks,

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:35.630
I'll talk a little bit about
one of our other very major

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:40.730
problems, which crept
up on us so slowly

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:44.100
that we didn't realize how
bad it was going to be.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:46.100
But we had leaks.

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:51.590
Now, nitrogen tetraoxide
is a nasty material.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:54.320
If it finds a small
leak, the small leak

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.480
soon gets to be a bigger leak.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:01.590
And it's toxic, so you
really don't want a leak.

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:06.440
And we had designed everything
so light and slender

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:10.730
that we had leaks
around these ports

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:13.280
at the bottom of the--
particularly the descent stage

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.260
tanks.

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:20.010
And we worked hard at this.

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:24.350
In fact, we wound up
changing all the seals.

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:28.340
And in fact, one of the vehicles
that was already at the Cape

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:32.570
had to send back this
whole lower structure

00:25:32.570 --> 00:25:35.900
so that we could redo the seals.

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:38.660
It was a terribly
frustrating affair.

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:41.390
And we were looking
for leaks that

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:47.180
were on the order of 1
cubic centimeter per year.

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:49.150
Pretty small leak.

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:52.250
With jet fuel, you would
never worry about that.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:06.880
Well, then this is a picture in
our final assembly area, where

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:14.160
we have an accent stage that's
being supported independently

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:16.380
of the descent stage.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:20.580
And it shows you some of the
precautions we took with some

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:23.220
of the thinner gauge materials.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:29.720
And some of these
structural gauges--

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:33.060
say, in this shear web--

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:34.260
were really quite thin.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:38.310
We got down to ten thousandths.

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:40.980
We would chem mill
down to ten thousandths

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.960
and keep the extra
thickness around the edges

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:46.800
where the connectors were.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:49.840
And we tried going to 008.

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:54.360
And then we counted the number
of grains across a 008 section

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.250
and chickened out.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:27:00.300
We went to the ten thousandths.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:02.880
I might add that we
chem milled almost

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.920
everything you can think of.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:07.500
Propellant tubing
was chem milled,

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:11.910
keeping the original thickness
where the connectors were.

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:19.470
And I can't offhand say how
many man hours were expended

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:23.250
in trying to save weight,
but it was a large number,

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:25.365
and it was an endless process.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:36.340
Now, this is a picture of
the ascent stage in sort

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:38.950
of a stripped condition.

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:42.790
Can see the ascent
propellant tank.

00:27:42.790 --> 00:27:46.270
You can see its
support out here.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:49.900
This is a good picture of the
window, which is just inches

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:54.730
from the astronaut's eye.

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:57.340
And you can also
see that we have

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:02.320
the separate tanks for the
reaction control system.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:05.390
And that was one system that
was completely redundant.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:09.610
We had a set of tanks on
either side of the machine.

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:15.190
And these tanks were
positive expulsion.

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.530
In other words, there
was a bladder in them

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:19.840
that allowed the
pressure to surely

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.690
drive the propellant out.

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:25.570
And of course, we used the
reaction control system

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:28.660
not only for maneuvering,
but to settle

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:32.950
the propellant in the main tanks
before a main engine firing.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:46.560
Now, this was the next step
in the assembly of the asset

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:47.790
stage.

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:51.190
And for those of you who
are familiar with airplanes,

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:53.980
this is an inside-out machine.

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:58.980
In other words, in an airplane,
you get the sleek exterior,

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:02.040
and then you package
everything inside it as densely

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:04.980
as you can, as
some people say, so

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.470
that even if you dropped
a broken egg into it,

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:09.430
it wouldn't drain through.

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:11.820
But here, we had a
machine that didn't

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:14.370
have to be
aerodynamically slick,

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:19.050
so we built it inside-out.

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:22.170
Much of the wiring--

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:27.210
much of the equipment were
outside the pressure vessel.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:30.370
And you can see some
of the wiring here.

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:33.360
And then, on the
outside, we're going

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:36.780
to have a thermal shield
which also doubled

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:39.450
as a micro meteoroid bumper.

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:42.390
And that had to be
supported somehow.

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:47.250
And that led to this
birdcage-like affair,

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:52.270
which supported this skin.

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:57.690
Now, this-- you might ask, well,
how come some parts are shiny

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:59.505
and some parts are black?

00:29:59.505 --> 00:30:05.220
Well, let me just say that a
very extensive thermal analysis

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:10.110
was done, because we
were trying to minimize

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:13.050
the power necessary
in this vehicle

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:16.200
to cut down the amount
of batteries that

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:21.120
had to be carried, and also
to cut down the amount of heat

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:26.400
that had to be put overboard.

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:30.750
And this is the result
of some tests and both--

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:35.070
well, analysis as well as tests
in a thermal vacuum chamber.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:44.530
This is a better view of the
reaction control thruster.

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:47.865
You see, there are four--

00:30:47.865 --> 00:30:53.030
a similar [? quad ?] on
each corner of the vehicle.

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:57.050
One of the things that was
added very late in the game--

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:59.750
and which is not
on my model here--

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:04.370
is this shield which
protects the descent stage

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:06.920
from the firing of
the reaction control

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:08.375
thruster that's pointing down.

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:13.010
This was the result
of some tests

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:17.630
fairly late in the program
that were done on the reaction

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:22.190
control thrusters at the
manned spaceflight center.

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:33.030
This is another view of
the final assembly area.

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:37.320
This is an ascent stage that
is substantially complete

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:39.990
at the front end,
but hasn't been all

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:41.920
put together on the back end.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:44.820
And the reason for showing
this is to show you

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:46.710
that the equipment--

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:52.830
batteries, radios-- were
mounted on coal rails.

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:57.330
The coal rails had glycol
circulating in them.

00:31:57.330 --> 00:32:00.750
The glycol heated water.

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:05.190
The water went overboard
through a porous plate

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:08.640
so that, in effect,
ice was formed,

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:10.665
and it sublimed into space.

00:32:10.665 --> 00:32:13.770
And that was the only
way we could get heat out

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:18.010
of the vehicle if we
had too much heat.

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:21.640
This also shows some
of the paperwork.

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:24.550
The weight of the vehicle--

00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:28.440
I'm not sure whether it weighed
more than the paper or not,

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:34.420
but we had extensive
pedigrees on just about

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:36.640
everything you can think of.

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:39.670
And that's a very
important thing,

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:42.440
because way back
in the beginning,

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:47.560
we worried about reliability.

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.890
At that time,
aircraft experience

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:54.240
was not particularly attractive.

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:56.830
It didn't give you
any great assurance.

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:58.990
And one of our people
came up with the idea

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:05.950
that there should be no such
thing as a random failure.

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:07.870
If you have something
that doesn't work,

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:12.160
either a ground
test or any test,

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:16.340
you ought to be able to find
out what it is and fix it.

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:18.010
And in the course
of 10 years, we

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:23.860
had something over 14,000
such anomaly reports,

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:29.830
and I believe only 22 escaped
satisfactory analysis.

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:34.540
In the case of those 22, we
changed something anyhow.

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:37.180
And I think it
was this attention

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:43.560
to the nasty little details
that made a difference.

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:45.080
I've mentioned tests.

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:49.470
I should point out
that we couldn't flight

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:50.840
test this vehicle.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:52.410
It's made only to fly in space.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:58.710
We couldn't hot fire it
because our experience

00:33:58.710 --> 00:34:01.410
with the propellants
was that you really

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:06.850
couldn't clean the system
adequately if you used it.

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:09.929
In other words, the
propellers would tend to gum

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:13.090
up the solenoid
valves and so forth.

00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:17.550
So each vehicle that was
launched from Cape Kennedy

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:23.550
was an unused, untested
vehicle in every sense

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:25.780
except electrically.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:27.840
We were able to do a
rather thorough job

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:30.810
in electrical checkout.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:40.679
But it did lead to the process
of setting up a very complex--

00:34:40.679 --> 00:34:42.449
and I should tell you--

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:46.139
steadily evolving
ground test system

00:34:46.139 --> 00:34:48.480
so that we could
assure ourselves

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.969
that the equipment
we were getting

00:34:50.969 --> 00:34:54.870
was good, that it would work
after it was in the vehicle,

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:56.070
and so on and so forth.

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:09.620
This is a photo from
the final assembly area

00:35:09.620 --> 00:35:15.440
again, showing a complete
vehicle except for the landing

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:17.060
gear here.

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:19.450
And same thing here.

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:22.250
And the point here
is simply that we

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:27.440
were working toward a
three-month on-center launch

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:28.320
schedule.

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:32.260
So we had to have these vehicles
coming along fairly regularly.

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:42.840
This photograph
shows a descent stage

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:46.560
after the landing gear is on.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:48.240
The principal reason
for showing this

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:57.590
is that we had a rather
elaborate blanket of insulation

00:35:57.590 --> 00:36:02.060
there to protect the descent
stage when the ascent

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:03.290
stage took off.

00:36:06.730 --> 00:36:10.240
We tried to run some tests
in the vacuum chambers

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:13.770
that NASA had at White Sands.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:20.610
And we could run short bursts at
different levels of separation,

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:25.710
but we had no way of running
a truly dynamic test.

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:30.590
And in fact, we
never really knew

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:33.860
what happened until the
very last mission, when

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:40.400
the TV camera on the rover
was aimed at the takeoff.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:43.220
And while this is a
still, a lot of stuff

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:47.660
went flying out sideways
from that insulation blanket.

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:51.040
But the vehicle just pops up.

00:36:51.040 --> 00:36:53.180
Ascent stage pops up.

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:56.270
Now, I should point
out that we have

00:36:56.270 --> 00:37:03.140
a fly-by-wire vertical takeoff
and vertical landing machine.

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:06.620
In the landing condition,
it's like a transport

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:08.600
because it's heavy.

00:37:08.600 --> 00:37:11.900
And when it gets back to
dock with the command module,

00:37:11.900 --> 00:37:14.000
it's like a [? fighter, ?]
because it's

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.870
gone through a 10-to-1
reduction in weight.

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:26.770
I've mentioned people
earlier in what I've said,

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:31.525
but it's impossible to do
credit to all the people

00:37:31.525 --> 00:37:33.030
who were involved.

00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:35.980
Or for that matter, all the
families that were involved.

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:39.730
We worked a lot of
people very, very hard.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.900
And this is the check-out
group at the Cape.

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:45.380
And I'll read this for you.

00:37:45.380 --> 00:37:48.310
This is for the last mission.

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:53.590
"This may be our last,
but it will be our best."

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:57.790
And by that, they meant
fewer anomalies to clean up.

00:37:57.790 --> 00:38:01.870
And of course, the
lunar module did work.

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:08.440
And I think that I'd like
to use the view graph now

00:38:08.440 --> 00:38:09.190
for a minute.

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:12.220
We could turn off the
slides, and talk a little bit

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:18.170
about something that I
fell into by accident--

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:19.698
namely, management.

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:30.430
This is a program
organization chart,

00:38:30.430 --> 00:38:35.020
something that our
customer really wanted.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:38.300
They wanted to know
who was doing what.

00:38:38.300 --> 00:38:39.430
And we put this together.

00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:44.530
Now, the trouble with this is
that this is a static chart.

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:49.430
It suggests that all of the
people at one level are equal.

00:38:49.430 --> 00:38:54.160
And I've sometimes thought that
an organization chart should

00:38:54.160 --> 00:38:59.530
look like a slowly-swirling
galaxy of stars--

00:38:59.530 --> 00:39:03.040
some that are brightening
and some that are dimming--

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:07.640
because the roles tend to
change as time goes by.

00:39:07.640 --> 00:39:09.700
The other thing is
that I would point out

00:39:09.700 --> 00:39:14.830
is that these people
never admitted that they

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:17.600
were on the third level.

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:21.820
Grumman had, I think,
a peculiar culture--

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:24.100
certainly in the time
that I was there--

00:39:24.100 --> 00:39:30.110
in that everybody looked at
the company as "my company,"

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:33.260
and they were not adverse
to calling anybody up

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:36.410
to tell them what should
be done differently,

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:38.300
or what was going on.

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:41.840
And it led to a certain
amount of confusion sometimes,

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:44.750
but the word got
around very quickly.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:50.150
And I will say that,
for many years,

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:53.240
the company never had
an organization chart.

00:39:53.240 --> 00:39:56.700
Roy Grumman, who founded the
company, was [? against ?] it,

00:39:56.700 --> 00:40:00.780
so we didn't have
it for many years.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:04.040
The other thing that
I would point out here

00:40:04.040 --> 00:40:07.570
is how the manpower
was expended.

00:40:07.570 --> 00:40:10.550
And there are a couple of
interesting points here.

00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:16.670
We were always under criticism
for running too much overtime.

00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:19.340
But it's much more effective--

00:40:19.340 --> 00:40:23.630
in my view-- to run overtime
than to try to add people,

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:25.860
in certain cases.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:29.540
The other thing we ran
into was that these slopes

00:40:29.540 --> 00:40:35.940
right in here, I think,
were maximum achievable.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:42.000
You can bring people on board
a major program just so fast.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:48.380
Otherwise, you wind up with
people not being productive.

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:51.830
In fact, a good argument,
what happens in major programs

00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:57.590
frequently is that the end
date-- or in this case,

00:40:57.590 --> 00:41:00.080
the lunar landing date--

00:41:00.080 --> 00:41:02.000
was fixed.

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:05.720
And there's a tendency for
the start date to creep.

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:10.160
And of course, the
effort is to build up

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:15.210
as fast as you can in
order to get the job done.

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:25.260
This slope right about here
is 1,750 people per year.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:28.850
Now, that's an
instantaneous number.

00:41:28.850 --> 00:41:32.660
But at the real
peak, we're adding

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.630
about 3,000 people a year.

00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:39.080
And that comes about
because there's

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:40.940
an acceleration involved.

00:41:40.940 --> 00:41:45.230
And by the time we
got here into '65,

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:47.150
we thought we knew
what we were doing,

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:49.580
and could bring people onboard.

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:51.350
And this is particularly
true in bringing

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:53.330
subcontractors onboard.

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:58.130
About half of the effort
went to major subcontractors,

00:41:58.130 --> 00:42:01.180
and some smaller subcontractors.

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:07.120
This gap right here
represents the effort

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:10.330
to fireproof everything
that came after the fire

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.510
at the Cape.

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:14.970
And then you see we resumed.

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:18.810
What happens here, of course,
is that there are some things

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:22.080
that you can stop, and
there are other things that

00:42:22.080 --> 00:42:23.340
just have to keep going.

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:28.840
So you can't really just
come way down here somewhere.

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:31.970
And then, of course,
once you have

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:35.470
reached an operational
stage, things

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:36.750
go downhill pretty rapidly.

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:50.405
Well, I'd like to think a
couple of summary remarks.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:58.490
We had the advantage of
a long-term commitment.

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:02.990
There are not many
programs that have

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:06.800
had that kind of advantage.

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:09.440
And I give Mr.
Kennedy great credit

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:13.700
for having the gumption
to stand up and say,

00:43:13.700 --> 00:43:16.100
"before this decade
is out," and so forth.

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:26.790
I think it was important that
we recognized our priorities.

00:43:26.790 --> 00:43:30.530
And in a highly-technical
undertaking,

00:43:30.530 --> 00:43:34.250
I think there's no question
in my mind that [? what ?]

00:43:34.250 --> 00:43:37.130
you have to do the job right.

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:41.170
If you take shortcuts,
you pay more--

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:45.410
you pay a larger penalty later.

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:50.450
I mentioned the idea of no
such thing as a random failure.

00:43:50.450 --> 00:43:52.520
It worked for us.

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:56.810
Some of our people likened
it to turning every stone

00:43:56.810 --> 00:43:59.930
on the beach over to
see what was under it.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:03.440
It felt that way at times.

00:44:03.440 --> 00:44:07.850
We invested a tremendous
amount of effort on the ground

00:44:07.850 --> 00:44:10.640
simply because we
couldn't flight test.

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:13.970
And some of those ideas
went over into our aircraft

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:15.410
experience later on.

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:20.600
We found we could save time and
effort in developing aircraft

00:44:20.600 --> 00:44:23.460
if we did more
work on the ground

00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:28.740
and didn't try to fly at
the earliest possible date.

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:34.370
It also taught us what I call
the basic program dilemma.

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:38.030
And that is that if you're
going to do something truly

00:44:38.030 --> 00:44:44.870
innovative, you can't possibly
know what the schedule is

00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:47.870
and what the cost
is going to be.

00:44:47.870 --> 00:44:52.070
And if, conversely, you know
precisely what the schedule is,

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:54.980
and what the cost is, you
ought to stop and think

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:58.370
whether it's worth doing,
because it may be obsolete

00:44:58.370 --> 00:45:00.275
before you start.

00:45:00.275 --> 00:45:02.150
Now, this is something
that I think engineers

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:04.790
understand quite well.

00:45:04.790 --> 00:45:06.980
I've had a terrible time
with some congressmen

00:45:06.980 --> 00:45:07.730
on that subject.

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:10.135
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLING]

00:45:10.135 --> 00:45:11.840
GAVIN: The other
thing I would mention

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:17.780
is that in being in
Europe and in Japan--

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:19.910
and more recently, in China--

00:45:19.910 --> 00:45:23.240
the impact of Apollo
has been greater

00:45:23.240 --> 00:45:26.720
overseas than it has
been in this country.

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:30.590
The American public is pretty
blasé about these things.

00:45:30.590 --> 00:45:36.330
It's assumed that the rocket
scientists can do anything.

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:38.390
Well, they almost can.

00:45:38.390 --> 00:45:43.670
But the American public
passes on to something else.

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:48.950
But we created a
reputation overseas

00:45:48.950 --> 00:45:52.250
that people are still
trying to catch up with.

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:54.860
And I think this
is something that--

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:58.700
particularly those of you who
are students should understand.

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:06.990
That the Apollo program was
a tremendous sales effort

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:10.440
for American technology.

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:14.240
And finally, last comment.

00:46:14.240 --> 00:46:17.360
A lot of people talk
about systems engineering.

00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:20.540
It means a lot of things
to a lot of people.

00:46:20.540 --> 00:46:22.980
In some cases, it's
just a matter of,

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:28.020
do you get all the interface
signals correct, and so on.

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:31.310
But I think that anybody who's
associated with designing

00:46:31.310 --> 00:46:33.860
flying machines--
whether they're flying

00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:37.940
in the atmosphere, or in
orbit, or outer space--

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:43.880
really is faced with doing
a superior job of systems

00:46:43.880 --> 00:46:45.350
engineering.

00:46:45.350 --> 00:46:49.100
And the reason is that if
you don't do a superior job,

00:46:49.100 --> 00:46:54.200
the results are catastrophic,
and wind up on page one.

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:59.390
And you really can't
afford to have that happen.

00:46:59.390 --> 00:47:02.090
I grew up as an
engineer in a company

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:07.790
that was founded by a pilot,
and [? so ?] some years

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:12.765
were some very senior
pilots in the management.

00:47:12.765 --> 00:47:15.590
In the airplane
days, it was simply

00:47:15.590 --> 00:47:19.700
"bring the pilot back
one way or another."

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:21.180
And we did that.

00:47:21.180 --> 00:47:25.160
And I think that if you have
that in mind when you're

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:28.340
involved with designing
flying machines,

00:47:28.340 --> 00:47:34.100
that you will do a superior
job of systems engineering.

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:35.630
Now, sometimes,
you wonder, well,

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:38.960
can you apply that
to other effort?

00:47:38.960 --> 00:47:42.320
Well, in a selective
way, you can.

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:48.260
Those little US Postal Service
trucks you see running around

00:47:48.260 --> 00:47:52.070
are the result of applying
some of the lessons that

00:47:52.070 --> 00:47:54.830
came out of this program.

00:47:54.830 --> 00:47:57.200
And that's a whole 'nother
story, so I'll stop there,

00:47:57.200 --> 00:47:59.331
and I'll be happy to
take any questions.

00:47:59.331 --> 00:48:01.786
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:11.545
Yes.

00:48:11.545 --> 00:48:13.920
AUDIENCE: When you get to the
end of a project like this,

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:16.800
I think you often learn
a lot along the way,

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:18.980
and sometimes think you
could have done something

00:48:18.980 --> 00:48:20.062
differently.

00:48:20.062 --> 00:48:21.770
Was there anything
you learned at the end

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:23.070
that you wish you
knew at the beginning,

00:48:23.070 --> 00:48:24.726
and you would've done anything
differently because of that?

00:48:24.726 --> 00:48:26.820
GAVIN: Well, I can
certainly tell you

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:28.600
that if I were doing
it today, there are

00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:30.644
some things I'd do differently.

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:37.840
At the time that we
designed the lunar module,

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:40.090
we were very
restricted on where we

00:48:40.090 --> 00:48:43.250
could use solid-state devices.

00:48:43.250 --> 00:48:45.190
In fact, all of the
communication gear

00:48:45.190 --> 00:48:48.520
was made up of
discrete elements.

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:51.370
And I would
certainly avoid that.

00:48:51.370 --> 00:48:57.370
That was a nightmare,
getting enough units

00:48:57.370 --> 00:49:00.370
that would pass test.

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:04.480
I would also have
a digital data bus,

00:49:04.480 --> 00:49:09.460
just to reduce the miles and
miles of wire that we had.

00:49:09.460 --> 00:49:15.940
We deliberately went to
a skinnier gauge of wire

00:49:15.940 --> 00:49:18.850
than is commonly used
in aircraft in order

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:20.440
to save weight.

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:25.300
And we paid for it, because
we had ever so many faults

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:30.070
that came about from just plain
handling the bundles of wire.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:33.460
So anything you
could do to get rid

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:38.230
of the old-fashioned way of
wiring would be an advantage.

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:46.370
I think that it's
also quite likely

00:49:46.370 --> 00:49:50.090
that a lot of the equipment
that we had-- well, supposing we

00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:55.890
take the MIT-designed guidance
and navigation equipment.

00:49:55.890 --> 00:50:00.560
I think that can all be done
in a miniaturized version today

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:04.070
that would weigh a lot less.

00:50:04.070 --> 00:50:07.580
I'm not sure we'd have
GPS in lunar orbit,

00:50:07.580 --> 00:50:13.610
but I'm sure that
what we had, while it

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:16.280
was great for its
time, is something

00:50:16.280 --> 00:50:18.100
we could do much better today.

00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:20.831
And that's probably true
of almost all equipment.

00:50:23.660 --> 00:50:24.690
Larry?

00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:26.190
AUDIENCE: Were you
made aware of any

00:50:26.190 --> 00:50:28.482
of the parallel developments
in the [INAUDIBLE] Russia,

00:50:28.482 --> 00:50:30.458
the [INAUDIBLE] LM?

00:50:30.458 --> 00:50:33.280
GAVIN: Not a word.

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:36.170
No, I knew nothing about it.

00:50:36.170 --> 00:50:39.450
And I think I can safely
speak for the rest of the crew

00:50:39.450 --> 00:50:40.710
that we knew nothing about it.

00:50:40.710 --> 00:50:43.120
AUDIENCE: If I could have--
[? and ?] another question.

00:50:43.120 --> 00:50:45.460
The success of the LM,
among other things,

00:50:45.460 --> 00:50:47.940
was the watershed in
flight simulation.

00:50:47.940 --> 00:50:49.690
Does it work the
first time it was

00:50:49.690 --> 00:50:51.370
used in its environment
based entirely

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:53.342
upon simulated training?

00:50:53.342 --> 00:50:58.180
Can you tell us a little
bit about how that worked,

00:50:58.180 --> 00:51:00.100
and how you had
confidence that this

00:51:00.100 --> 00:51:03.520
could [INAUDIBLE] first time?

00:51:03.520 --> 00:51:08.700
GAVIN: Well, you have to
recognize that 40 years ago, I

00:51:08.700 --> 00:51:11.400
was a lot bolder
than I am today.

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:14.950
And so were we all.

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:17.910
But there were some lessons
learned in simulation.

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:21.930
In fact, when we built the
X-29A, the little airplane that

00:51:21.930 --> 00:51:28.380
had the forward short wings and
the huge negative stability,

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:31.410
we treated that
in simulation much

00:51:31.410 --> 00:51:33.540
as we did the lunar module.

00:51:33.540 --> 00:51:36.990
And the pilot came back from
the first flight and said, gee,

00:51:36.990 --> 00:51:39.420
it flies just like
the simulator.

00:51:39.420 --> 00:51:42.630
So there were some
lessons learned

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:46.740
during the lunar module.

00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:50.230
I crashed one of the
simulators several times

00:51:50.230 --> 00:51:51.660
for a very simple reason.

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:58.900
If you try to translate along
the surface of the moon,

00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:00.670
there's no air resistance.

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:04.860
So in order to stop, you had to
fire in the opposite direction.

00:52:04.860 --> 00:52:07.440
You don't automatically
slow down.

00:52:07.440 --> 00:52:11.100
Now, that's a thing that is
so counter-intuitive that it

00:52:11.100 --> 00:52:13.980
takes training to do well.

00:52:16.698 --> 00:52:17.610
Yes, Bob.

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:20.970
AUDIENCE: Joe, you were dealing
with a lot of different people

00:52:20.970 --> 00:52:23.445
within Grumman and within NASA.

00:52:26.608 --> 00:52:30.460
First of all, how did
you spend your time?

00:52:30.460 --> 00:52:35.290
Who did you work with the
most, and what kind of problems

00:52:35.290 --> 00:52:39.220
did you get into from a
management [? stance? ?]

00:52:39.220 --> 00:52:41.670
GAVIN: Well, I'll answer
the last one first.

00:52:41.670 --> 00:52:45.870
Every problem you
could possibly imagine.

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:51.510
In the very beginning, there
was a certain resentment among

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:58.980
the dyed-in-the-wool aircraft
people that this strange group

00:52:58.980 --> 00:53:05.220
had brought home a contract
that was going to take a lot

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:10.930
of the company's attention,
and people, and reserves.

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:16.930
So there was a matter initially
of getting the right people.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:20.620
And fortunately, I had
some excellent support

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:23.860
from people you know,
like Grant Hedrick,

00:53:23.860 --> 00:53:27.320
and from then-senior management.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:32.080
And so we did get
the right people.

00:53:32.080 --> 00:53:41.260
And of course, we had to manage
quite a number of somewhat

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:44.710
difficult subcontractors.

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:47.290
Joe Shea remembers
some of those days.

00:53:47.290 --> 00:53:52.330
He and I had a great debate one
time walking along the street

00:53:52.330 --> 00:54:00.820
down at the Cape as to how much
visibility the NASA people were

00:54:00.820 --> 00:54:06.550
going to have into
our subcontractors.

00:54:06.550 --> 00:54:09.790
And prior policy had been--

00:54:09.790 --> 00:54:12.370
in dealing with other
government agencies,

00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:16.520
is that we would manage
the subcontractors,

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:17.730
and that was that.

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:22.610
But he won his point, finally,
and we came down to the idea

00:54:22.610 --> 00:54:28.670
that NASA would have visibility
as to our relationships

00:54:28.670 --> 00:54:31.760
with the
subcontractors, but they

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:34.910
would communicate to
the subcontractors only

00:54:34.910 --> 00:54:38.630
through us, since we were
the prime contractor,

00:54:38.630 --> 00:54:44.514
and we felt very strongly that
we had the responsibility.

00:54:53.230 --> 00:54:55.410
I'm not sure I've answered
that question adequately,

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:58.990
but that gives you some idea.

00:54:58.990 --> 00:54:59.800
Yes, Todd.

00:54:59.800 --> 00:55:02.050
AUDIENCE: Joe, you talked
about 10 years of nostalgia,

00:55:02.050 --> 00:55:05.450
and the nostalgia over 10
years of frenzy and stress.

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:08.570
Can you share with us the memory
or two of a [? horror ?] story?

00:55:13.220 --> 00:55:19.110
GAVIN: Well-- oh, Clyde,
before I get to that,

00:55:19.110 --> 00:55:20.600
I want to go back to Bob.

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:22.610
There's one thing
I forgot to say,

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:27.350
and that is that I was
very fortunate in having

00:55:27.350 --> 00:55:33.650
a cadre of people with whom I
had worked for 10 or 15 years.

00:55:33.650 --> 00:55:38.950
So we were pretty well
calibrated one to the other.

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:42.440
And not only in the
engineering and the things,

00:55:42.440 --> 00:55:46.910
but I had a number
of good acquaintances

00:55:46.910 --> 00:55:49.820
amongst the technicians
and the foremen.

00:55:49.820 --> 00:55:54.260
And every day that
I was in Bethpage

00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:57.920
and not traveling to Houston or
some other [? garden ?] spot,

00:55:57.920 --> 00:56:01.310
I'd make it my business to
walk through the manufacturing

00:56:01.310 --> 00:56:05.810
area or the test area and talk
to the people who were busy

00:56:05.810 --> 00:56:06.990
there.

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:09.650
And it's amazing what
you can learn just

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:13.940
by taking an hour's walk
and seeing firsthand

00:56:13.940 --> 00:56:17.960
what people are doing, and
what problems they're having.

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:19.520
And some of the
problems are simple.

00:56:19.520 --> 00:56:25.490
I recall one where the
thermostat on the heating

00:56:25.490 --> 00:56:29.060
system was stuck,
and it was too hot.

00:56:29.060 --> 00:56:32.540
And apparently, the
foreman had complained.

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:34.160
Nothing had happened.

00:56:34.160 --> 00:56:35.630
Well, I made a phone call.

00:56:35.630 --> 00:56:36.410
It got fixed.

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:44.780
So the problems range
all over the place.

00:56:44.780 --> 00:56:47.480
I'll tell you that
we did occasionally

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:51.950
have to send people home,
because we discovered that some

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:56.630
of them were punching out at
the end of their 10- or 12-hour

00:56:56.630 --> 00:57:01.070
shift, and then coming
right back in and working

00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:02.990
another few hours.

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:06.110
And we had some people
who actually made

00:57:06.110 --> 00:57:10.100
themselves ill in the process.

00:57:10.100 --> 00:57:14.610
But everybody knew what
the name of the game was.

00:57:14.610 --> 00:57:18.530
And NASA did a great thing in
having the astronauts visit

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:23.780
the plant, oh, I think about
every second to third week.

00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:28.490
So it became a question of,
we are building this vehicle

00:57:28.490 --> 00:57:32.530
for someone we know,
not for some agency

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:37.390
that is a bunch of
bureaucrats somewhere.

00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:41.620
Now, back to Todd.

00:57:41.620 --> 00:57:47.830
Well, the low point in some
days was going to Houston

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:52.150
to make a report on how
we were doing and having

00:57:52.150 --> 00:57:54.580
to get up there and
say, well, we've

00:57:54.580 --> 00:57:57.280
lost another couple of weeks.

00:57:57.280 --> 00:58:04.106
And it's not easy, but
that's the way things were.

00:58:04.106 --> 00:58:08.950
And we did what we had to.

00:58:11.650 --> 00:58:14.860
Speaking of Houston, I
remember one such meeting

00:58:14.860 --> 00:58:21.970
where one of the fellows who
was involved in a navigation

00:58:21.970 --> 00:58:26.450
guidance group was
suffering from an earache.

00:58:26.450 --> 00:58:28.540
And I thought I
had arranged it so

00:58:28.540 --> 00:58:32.620
that he wouldn't go on that
trip, but he went anyhow.

00:58:32.620 --> 00:58:35.710
And he was under medication.

00:58:35.710 --> 00:58:37.510
And he got up to
the podium to talk

00:58:37.510 --> 00:58:40.930
about what they were
doing, and he was so

00:58:40.930 --> 00:58:46.510
relaxed from the
medication that he created

00:58:46.510 --> 00:58:48.500
a sensational impression.

00:58:48.500 --> 00:58:50.650
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLING]

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:54.390
GAVIN: But he knew what
he was talking about.

00:58:54.390 --> 00:58:56.080
He got away with that.

00:58:56.080 --> 00:59:00.250
And afterwards, somebody
asked me, what is he on?

00:59:00.250 --> 00:59:02.530
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLING]

00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:04.960
GAVIN: But that was
the kind of spirit

00:59:04.960 --> 00:59:08.920
that we had in the group, and
gives you a couple of ideas

00:59:08.920 --> 00:59:09.690
of what went on.

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:14.290
Yes, [? Errol? ?]

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:15.960
AUDIENCE: Joe, if
you had to reach back

00:59:15.960 --> 00:59:18.180
to your undergraduate
years and think

00:59:18.180 --> 00:59:20.490
about what you learned
in MIT, what carried you

00:59:20.490 --> 00:59:23.466
through your career?

00:59:23.466 --> 00:59:29.338
GAVIN: Well, that's a
hard question to answer,

00:59:29.338 --> 00:59:30.880
[? Errol, ?] because
you see, I never

00:59:30.880 --> 00:59:34.010
became an expert at anything.

00:59:34.010 --> 00:59:40.440
But I do remember very
clearly Professor Joe Newell,

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:46.720
who taught structures, and who
was a very conservative man.

00:59:46.720 --> 00:59:51.400
And when he went to
Washington, he took the train.

00:59:51.400 --> 00:59:53.080
And that made a
great impression.

00:59:53.080 --> 00:59:55.505
AUDIENCE: [CHUCKLING]

00:59:57.770 --> 01:00:00.150
GAVIN: Made a great impression.

01:00:00.150 --> 01:00:02.922
Yes, Stan.

01:00:02.922 --> 01:00:07.090
AUDIENCE: When you commented
on some of the things that

01:00:07.090 --> 01:00:11.992
might have transitioned into
your airplane activities,

01:00:11.992 --> 01:00:17.430
can you comment on maybe
the success and failures

01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:20.070
of translating the systems
engineering approaches

01:00:20.070 --> 01:00:25.800
that you used into
the aircraft business?

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:31.040
GAVIN: Well, the
question-- in case

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:35.670
you didn't hear it
back there-- was,

01:00:35.670 --> 01:00:39.060
were you able to translate
some of the systems engineering

01:00:39.060 --> 01:00:43.010
aspects of handling the LM back
into the aircraft business?

01:00:43.010 --> 01:00:45.840
Well, you have to
realize that we

01:00:45.840 --> 01:00:48.570
took what we had learned
in the aircraft business

01:00:48.570 --> 01:00:51.180
and used it in the LM.

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:54.840
And then, of course, we
had on-the-job training

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:58.140
because we ran into
so many difficulties.

01:00:58.140 --> 01:01:02.430
And it is true that
we did sharpen up

01:01:02.430 --> 01:01:06.840
the aircraft business
a bit after we

01:01:06.840 --> 01:01:08.850
space cadets took it over.

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:19.380
We instituted formal
pre-flight reviews.

01:01:19.380 --> 01:01:22.860
Now, there always had
been some sort of review

01:01:22.860 --> 01:01:27.120
prior to flying a new
airplane, but nothing like

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:30.210
what NASA had taught us to do.

01:01:30.210 --> 01:01:32.930
So we introduced that.

01:01:40.804 --> 01:01:42.640
That stands out in my--

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:44.350
and the simulation.

01:01:44.350 --> 01:01:47.320
That and the simulation
are the two big things.

01:01:47.320 --> 01:01:51.550
You see, at the time, we were
involved in some aircraft

01:01:51.550 --> 01:01:55.150
that were very highly--

01:01:55.150 --> 01:02:01.030
well, had a lot of
electronics in them, and where

01:02:01.030 --> 01:02:09.070
things like crosstalk
and mutual interference

01:02:09.070 --> 01:02:12.460
had really been worked
on very, very hard.

01:02:12.460 --> 01:02:20.190
So-- well, we did also
introduce the matter

01:02:20.190 --> 01:02:24.990
of certifying the people
who could open a connection.

01:02:29.370 --> 01:02:35.130
With all those
prongs in a plug, it

01:02:35.130 --> 01:02:39.210
takes real skill to
avoid damaging it.

01:02:39.210 --> 01:02:40.676
And that helped.

01:02:40.676 --> 01:02:45.390
But I think that some of the
aircraft we were building

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:49.660
at the time were just as
complicated-- in fact,

01:02:49.660 --> 01:02:52.620
some of them were more
complicated than the lunar

01:02:52.620 --> 01:02:53.980
module.

01:02:53.980 --> 01:03:00.240
As I recall, let's
see, the EA-6B

01:03:00.240 --> 01:03:04.960
had something like
80 antennas on it.

01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:09.440
So that was not all
that easy either.

